165
00:09:12,385 --> 00:09:15,221
"The great tall tailor always comes"

166
00:09:15,305 --> 00:09:17,265
"to little girls that suck their thumbs."

167
00:09:17,348 --> 00:09:19,100
Are you listening, Sasha? Right?

168
00:09:19,184 --> 00:09:21,144
"And ere they dream what he's about,"

169
00:09:21,227 --> 00:09:23,688
"he takes his great sharp scissors out"

170
00:09:23,813 --> 00:09:26,066
"and then cuts their thumbs clean off."

171
00:09:26,149 --> 00:09:27,293
Dwight, Dwight... There's a photo.

172
00:09:27,317 --> 00:09:28,461
What the hell are you reading to them?

173
00:09:28,485 --> 00:09:29,879
These are cautionary tales for kids.

174
00:09:29,903 --> 00:09:30,963
My <i>grom-mutter</i> used to read them...

175
00:09:30,987 --> 00:09:32,214
Yeah, you know what? No, no, no, no.

176
00:09:32,238 --> 00:09:34,532
They... No. The kids don't wanna hear some weirdo book

177
00:09:34,616 --> 00:09:36,868
that your Nazi war criminal grandmother gave you.

178
00:09:36,951 --> 00:09:38,870
What's a Nazi? What's a Nazi?

179
00:09:38,953 --> 00:09:40,431
Nazi was a fascist movement... Don't...

180
00:09:40,455 --> 00:09:42,600
From the 1930s in Germany... Don't, don't talk about Nazis

181
00:09:42,624 --> 00:09:44,894
in front of... You know what? They're gonna have nightmares.

182
00:09:44,918 --> 00:09:45,978
Why don't you just shut it?

183
00:09:46,002 --> 00:09:49,255
I was gonna teach the children how to make cornhusk dolls.

184
00:09:49,339 --> 00:09:52,759
Why don't you just leave, okay? Okay.

185
00:09:53,927 --> 00:09:56,721
Bye, Mr. Poop. All right. There goes Mr. Poop.

186
00:09:56,805 --> 00:09:58,723
Now, who likes Dane Cook?

187
00:09:58,807 --> 00:10:00,058
I do! I do!

188
00:10:00,141 --> 00:10:02,936
Children cannot lie. They are innocent and they speak the truth.

189
00:10:03,019 --> 00:10:05,355
And out of the mouths of babes,

190
00:10:05,438 --> 00:10:08,108
Michael Scott is freaking cool.

191
00:10:14,739 --> 00:10:17,075
You know, I never misbehaved in front of my father

192
00:10:17,158 --> 00:10:20,036
because he was a very strict disciplinarian.

193
00:10:20,578 --> 00:10:23,957
I can only hope my mate has some of those same qualities.

194
00:10:29,045 --> 00:10:32,298
This is where the magic happens. Right over here.

195
00:10:32,382 --> 00:10:33,716
Let me show you this.

196
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:37,846
See all these? You know what that is? That's paper.

197
00:10:37,929 --> 00:10:40,974
This is where paper comes from. Any questions?

198
00:10:41,182 --> 00:10:44,144
So, you cut the paper and dye it and stuff?

199
00:10:44,310 --> 00:10:47,230
No, we don't actually cut the paper. That's a good question.

200
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:51,651
The paper is sent to us, cut and dyed, from a paper manufacturer.

201
00:10:51,776 --> 00:10:55,780
And then we sell it to a business for more than we paid for it.

202
00:10:55,864 --> 00:10:57,115
That's not fair. No.

203
00:10:57,198 --> 00:10:59,200
Yes, it is. Well... No.

204
00:10:59,284 --> 00:11:01,286
You need someone in the middle to facilitate...

205
00:11:01,369 --> 00:11:03,329
You're just a middleman. I'm not just a middleman.

206
00:11:03,413 --> 00:11:06,332
Wait. Why doesn't the sawmill just sell the paper directly to people?

207
00:11:06,416 --> 00:11:08,710
You are describing Office Depot.

208
00:11:08,793 --> 00:11:10,712
And they're kind of running us out of business.

209
00:11:10,795 --> 00:11:13,006
We have better service than they do.

210
00:11:15,175 --> 00:11:16,968
There's Creed! Let's take a look

211
00:11:17,051 --> 00:11:19,220
